  "id": "feeling-unwell",
  "uuid": "7b9f6705-d377-5ceb-ab63-2595bf3f5e1d",
  "title": "Feeling Unwell: Head, Stomach and a Fever",
  "category": "health",
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "level": 3,
  "order": 304,
  "theme": "health",
  "culturalNote": "\"Pole!\" (sorry) is the first thing any Kenyan says when you're unwell — and they'll say it again and again. Body parts are a great example of singular and plural pairs: mkono / mikono (arm / arms), mguu / miguu (leg / legs), jicho / macho (eye / eyes). In everyday Kenyan speech \"-inauma\" (it hurts) works for every body part: kichwa inauma, tumbo inauma. Clinics are everywhere, and \"kliniki\" is the word people use.",
  "startingPoint": "10 a.m. at the office. Achieng notices you've had your head on your desk for a while.",
  "grammarFocus": [
    "ko-location",
    "present-na",
    "time-swahili"
  ],
